# FestFlow — Cursor Rules

You are assisting on FestFlow, a Next.js 15 volunteer coordination system for college fests, built under a 36-hour hackathon deadline. Architectural discipline matters more than cleverness. Follow these rules strictly.

## Project context
- Stack: Next.js 15 App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, Supabase (auth + Postgres + RLS), Zod, react-hook-form, date-fns, react-big-calendar
- LLM provider: runtime-selected from ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, GEMINI_API_KEY, or OPENAI_API_KEY — never hardcode a provider
- Notifications: ConsoleNotifier only for now, swappable to Resend later via the Notifier interface
- Auth: deferred to the end; use the dev role switcher pattern until Commit C20
- Build order is defined in TODO.md — respect it, do not implement future commits early

## Shell command handling
Cursor's terminal integration is unreliable on this machine (Git Bash on Windows). Follow these rules:
- Do not run npm install, npx, git, supabase, or any long-running command
- Instead, tell the human: "Run this in your terminal: <command>"
- Wait for the human to confirm the command succeeded before continuing
- The human handles all git operations (add, commit, push)
- If a task requires shell execution, STOP and give a clear command block, then wait

## Non-negotiable architectural rules

### The engine is the only thing that assigns volunteers
- `reconcileTask` in `lib/assignment/engine.ts` is the sole authority for creating `assignments` rows with status 'assigned' or 'waitlist'
- API routes call `reconcileTask` after any capacity-affecting change
- Never let the LLM, UI, or any other code path directly insert assignment rows
- Humans can only: create/edit/delete tasks, approve/reject volunteers, drop assignments

### The LLM never writes to the database
- LLM outputs are proposals, not actions
- Option A (command parser): LLM returns structured intent → deterministic code dispatches to existing API routes
- Option C (explanation): LLM returns a string attached to an already-made decision
- Option D (conflict resolver): deterministic code generates the move pool, LLM returns indices, deterministic code executes

### The Notifier never runs inside the engine
- `reconcileTask` returns `{ filled, waitlisted, notifications: Notification[] }`
- The API route calls `getNotifier().send(notifications)` after the engine returns

### RBAC is a single source of truth
- All permission checks route through `lib/rbac/permissions.ts` `can(role, action)`
- Server routes use `requireRole()` from `lib/rbac/guard.ts`
- Sidebar nav items filter via `can()`

### Provider-agnostic LLM access
- All LLM calls go through `getLLMClient()` which returns an `LLMClient` interface
- Never import `@anthropic-ai/sdk` or `@google/generative-ai` outside `lib/ai/providers/`
- Structured outputs use Zod schemas validated after the provider returns
- Every LLM call has a try/catch with a deterministic fallback

## File organization
- Pure logic goes in `lib/*` — no React, no Next.js imports
- Server-only code uses `lib/supabase/server.ts`; client code uses `lib/supabase/client.ts`
- API routes are thin: validate → call lib functions → return JSON
- Server components fetch data; client components handle interaction. 'use client' only when necessary
- Don't modify shadcn components directly — extend via wrappers

## Code style
- TypeScript strict. No `any` — use `unknown` and narrow
- Zod schemas colocated with their usage
- Dates: always date-fns + ISO 8601 strings at API boundaries
- Errors in API routes: `Response.json({ error: string }, { status: 4xx })`. Never throw raw.
- No server actions — all mutations go through API routes

## Database
- Migrations numbered and additive
- Every mutation goes through Supabase client
- RLS is permissive in dev; mark tightening work with `// TODO(rls):` comments
- Assignment status enforced at DB (check constraint) and Zod levels — keep in sync

## UI
- All forms use react-hook-form + Zod
- Toasts via sonner — no alert() or window.confirm()
- Loading states: skeletons for fetches, spinners for mutations
- Calendar events are derived from tasks — pure view, no independent state

## Commit discipline
- One commit per TODO.md checkbox
- Conventional commits: `feat(scope):`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `docs:`
- Tick the box in TODO.md as part of the next commit
- Split commits that exceed 300 lines of diff

## What NOT to do
- Do not add features not in TODO.md — suggest adding to "Future features" instead
- Do not implement email until explicitly requested
- Do not add drag-to-reschedule on calendar
- Do not build admin pages beyond stubs until future phase
- Do not add venues, participants, or certificates during core build
- Do not use localStorage except for the dev role switcher
- Do not install additional UI libraries
- Do not add tests during hackathon except for scoreVolunteer sanity checks
- Do not wrap everything in try/catch — catch only where there's a real fallback

## When a request would violate a rule
Stop and flag it: "This conflicts with [rule X] because [reason]. The intended pattern is [Y]. Want me to do it that way, or is there a reason to deviate?"

## Hard timebox on Option D
Commit C18.5/C18.6 has a hard timebox at hour 31. If fighting: `git revert`, stub the panel, move on. Do not let Option D cascade into lost auth or demo prep time.

## Dev role switcher contract
Until Commit C20, role is determined by:
- Client: `useDevRole()` hook reading from localStorage, default 'coordinator'
- Server: `getRoleServer(request)` reading `x-dev-role` header, default 'coordinator'

Both helpers MUST check `process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production'` and return null in production. Switcher UI renders only in dev.

## Swap points
- Console → Email: one line in `lib/notifications/index.ts`
- Admin feature: entry in `permissions.ts`, sidebar item, page
- Different LLM: different env var, no code change
- Smarter scoring: make `scoreVolunteer` async — engine loop unchanged

## Demo-critical
- Seed produces a demoable state: 1 coordinator, 10 approved volunteers, 3 pre-created tasks
- Console notifications must be readable at a glance
- The command "need 3 people for stage setup tomorrow 2-4pm, must know electrical" must parse reliably
- Calendar color-codes by fill status: green (full), amber (partial), red (empty)

## User context
The user is a CS undergraduate building this with a partner under real time pressure. Prioritize working code over elegant code, clear error messages, obvious next-step hints, and honest feedback if a request will eat more time than it's worth.
